-- The ruled §11.4 pattern, spelled with section binders: "for the purposes of
-- sections 1 and 2, foo means apple; for the purposes of sections 3 and 4, foo
-- means banana". Same-named binders in different sections are DISTINCT binders,
-- each read by its own section and its descendants — exactly as two ASSUMEs or
-- two definitions are.
--
-- The reader that reaches two of them at once is the ambiguity case, and lives
-- in not-ok/tc/section-given-ambiguous-parent.l4.
--
-- The spec spells the readers r1..r4; here they are `reader one`..`reader four`
-- and each carries an explicit GIVETH. Both changes are for the round-trip
-- check, which forbids any inference variable's rendering (@seed <> uniq@) from
-- appearing in prettyLayout's output and so reads a definition literally named
-- `r4` as a leaked gensym.

§ toplevel

DECLARE Fruit IS ONE OF Apple, Banana

§§ `1`
    GIVEN foo IS A Fruit

GIVETH A Fruit
`reader one` MEANS foo

§§ `2`
    GIVEN foo IS A Fruit

GIVETH A Fruit
`reader two` MEANS foo

§§ `3`
    GIVEN foo IS A Fruit

GIVETH A Fruit
`reader three` MEANS foo

§§ `4`
    GIVEN foo IS A Fruit

GIVETH A Fruit
`reader four` MEANS foo
